														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4834138">
									

<p>Hey Manhattan!</p>
<p>Got a bit of critique for you. First of all, I'll start with some of the more technical and grammatical issues.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>&quot;Observations have lead to believe that the clarity of the water is anomalouslyt obscrued from biological and mechanical vision.&quot; - It's anomalously obscured (minor spelling error). Also, you wouldn't say &quot;biological and mechanical vision&quot;, the word vision is barely ever used when it comes to machines or items. Usually the word &quot;detection&quot; might be used. The whole sentence is really wonky to read. It jumps all over the place in terms of being very formal (like saying biological and mechanical vision) to being extremely informal (lead to believe). Also, saying &quot;lead to believe&quot; without a clear reference to who is being lead to believe sounds really wonky as well.</p>
<p>&quot;Water from SCP-XXXX and Huka falls are chemically incompatible.&quot; Something being chemically incompatible means that they'll react if brought together (usually exothermically). Since I don't think that's the case here, a better term would be &quot;insolvent&quot;, if you mean that they simply don't mix.</p>
<p>&quot;Cross referencing has revealed that all fourteen unidentified species are related to avian, reptilic, and aquatic species.&quot; - You can't really &quot;cross reference&quot; when you're talking about animals. I even tried to look it up and found no reference to that term being used in conjunction with species.</p>
<p>Also, &quot;avian, reptilic and aquatic species&quot; is a really weird way to arrange species. Avian and Reptilian are both what is referred to as &quot;animal classes&quot;, a subdivision of the Chordata phylum. There is no &quot;aquatic&quot; animal class. There's three classes of fish, one class of amphibians, and mammals which are occasionally aquatic. So you have one animal class listed, then another animal class, and then a vague term that covers potentially 5 animal classes.</p>
<p>Another thing that really irked me, that line basically described 6 out of the 7 animal classes under the Chordata phylum, pretty much all animals except mammals. But then in the story, there's a horned rat.. a mammal. So you give a description that includes 6 out of the 7 animal classes, and then in the story the animal is actually from that 7th. class not included.</p>
<p>&quot;large increase of noise from human vocal cords.&quot; from the video log is also extremely wonky. First of all, you would say &quot;increase IN&quot;, not increase of. Secondly, when transcribing a video log, you'd never use the term &quot;noise from human vocal chords&quot;. You'd simply say &quot;Shouting&quot;, &quot;Screaming&quot;, &quot;Chanting&quot; or whatever else the people are doing. It's a much shorter word, and much more accurately describes the sounds being made. When you transcribe a video, your aim is always clarity and brevity. &quot;Noise from human vocal chords&quot; gives me virtually no information, especially compared to how much space it takes up.</p>
<p>If you want to aim for the more formal and scientific sounding tone, you really need a bit of research on the words you use.</p>
<p>Also, why isn't the Foundation keeping any kind of guard over these sites? What's preventing the Foundation from exploring them themselves?</p>
<p>About the story in general, I was a bit confused about who I'm rooting for here. Namid seems entirely passive and is quickly removed. Jack Bennett seems fairly aggressive and not entirely likeable from his monologues. I'm generally quite confused over what the real point of the story is. The SCP phenomena aren't really described in any kind of detail, and the people aren't either.</p>
<p>The premise itself could work, but the tone seems all wrong. The idea of a travelling agency that sells tickets to go to a number of SCPs that they've discovered, that could be a fun concept. The travel agency might be slightly clueless as to the exact nature of the SCPs, it could easily be a fun and light-hearted story.</p>
<p>When it's played straight, it doesn't really seem to work that well. The wondrous aspects are glossed over, and we're left with an angry man ranting on a boat.</p>
</div>
