					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/littlefieryone" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5419359); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=5419359&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606894928" alt="LittleFieryOne" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=5419359)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/littlefieryone" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5419359); return false;" >LittleFieryOne</a></span> <span class="odate time_1606888965 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">02 Dec 2020 06:02</span>
																	<br/>
						in discussion <a href="/forum/c-50742/per-page-discussions">Per Page Discussions / Per page discussions</a> &raquo;
						<a href="/scp-597/comments/show">SCP-597</a>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4834110">
									

<p>It's a -1 from me, but I want to add something to everything else that people have brought up.</p>
<p>I think this article's issues go farther than being tonal. To be frank, even ignoring the tone, it's just absurdly clunky to read. It's the use of punctuation more than anything. For example:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>597 is to be hosed down weekly, with special care given to the areas around the nipples, to prevent the buildup of saliva and drool.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The second comma feels&#8230; just randomly there. It's an unnecessary slice in the middle of the sentence. There's no reason to put that beat there. It would feel like a harmless typo if nearly every sentence didn't have the same or similar issues. And listen, I'm not just here to be a stickler about proper punctuation. Honestly, breaking grammar to get a certain emotion across is a part of writing. A Franz Kafka story often has a lot of commas for a lot of long sentences. But in his stories, it meshes with the word choice. It's hard to describe, but I find the language used in a Kafka story (admittedly barring the fact that most of us have only read translations) works with the punctuation. Here, even though some sentences use clinical language, that language is at war with how they're cut up and organized. The word choice is (sometimes) clinical; the sentence structure is not.</p>
<p>The tone has been defended at times by explaining how this article is supposed to feature Foundation personnel becoming enraptured with this SCP. However, since this structure problem is present throughout the article as opposed to gradually shifting into disorganized madness, to me it feels like the article is unpolished. And when that's combined with some of these monolithic paragraphs, it makes me just want to stop reading. My eyes start to roll into the back of my head.</p>
<p>I'm sorry if this is coming across as harsh, and in all fairness I'm likely not a saint when it comes to this as well. But in honesty, it feels weird that in this discussion, the closest others have come to describing this issue is bringing up the tone problem, which for me doesn't quite hit the nail on the head (which perhaps also suggests that I'm the only one bothered by this, so&#8230; oh well). Not angry or disappointed in anyone, but seeing that just really made me want to talk about it. As such, I also think that if this is gonna stay up, it definitely needs a rewrite. I know it's Series 1, but personally, I think even by that standard it falls flat.</p>

							</div>

								
					
